---
import Post from "../../../layouts/Post.astro";
import RunningRecordNav from "../../../components/RunningRecordNav.astro";
const frontmatter = {
  title: "Running record",
  description: "The dated history behind my running: races, training, longer outings and the conditions that make a comparison useful.",
  updated: "2026-10-09",
};
---
<Post frontmatter={frontmatter}>
  <RunningRecordNav />
  <p>
    This is the growing record behind my running. It reaches from childhood park
    races to the present return to the track, with the workouts, longer outings
    and memories that connect them. The <a href="/running-notebook/">notebook</a>
    holds the active project; these pages retain what happened as that project changes.
  </p>
  <p>
    For the current training, start with <a href="/running/record/2026/">the 2026
    return</a>. Its chronology and repeated-workout comparisons lead to the full
    dated notes. The <a href="/running/record/results/">results index</a> gives
    race marks and links back to their circumstances.
  </p>
  <h2>Periods</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><a href="/running/record/2026/">2026: returning to the track</a> —
      calibration, repeatable sessions and the independently earned LT and VO₂ progressions.</li>
    <li><a href="/running/record/2023-2025/">2023–2025: running beyond the track season</a> —
      intermittent training, the Orange County Marathon and later long runs.</li>
    <li><a href="/running/record/2022/">2022: the track season</a> —
      the recovered programme, time trials and men's and U20 national championships.</li>
    <li><a href="/running/record/2021/">2021: the 900s and the rest of the season</a> —
      twelve iterations of a familiar workout, the South Park 5 km best and the running between them.</li>
    <li><a href="/running/record/2020/">2020: short tests, track laps and longer runs</a> —
      summer marks, mixed-pace sessions, the Sofia short race and longer road outings.</li>
    <li><a href="/running/record/early/">2015–2019: the early record</a> —
      the neighbourhood loop, organized park races and the first exported runs.</li>
  </ul>
  <h2>How to read a record</h2>
  <p>
    Race results come from organizers or the athletics federation. Training times
    retain the repetition distance, recovery and circumstances recorded with them.
    A remembered effort keeps its uncertainty; a GPS distance remains a device
    measurement. These distinctions matter when two similar numbers describe
    different kinds of running.
  </p>
  <p>
    Each period opens with the story and selected dated details. Beneath them,
    monthly lists preserve all 375 running activity uploads in the supplied history,
    through 8 October 2026 locally. Warm-ups and cool-downs can have their own
    recordings; several uploads can belong to one outing. The lists therefore
    count recordings rather than physical training sessions. Earlier races and
    unrecorded memories can still have a place here.
  </p>
  <h2>Reading beyond the record</h2>
  <p>
    <a href="/what-should-a-running-workout-maximize/">What should a running workout
    maximize?</a> develops the questions behind my training. <a href="/running-makes-the-city-smaller/">Running
    makes the city smaller</a> follows a different outcome: the freedom to move
    through a city on foot. The record supplies the dated running behind both.
  </p>
  <p>
    New sessions will join the relevant period. A substantial change in the
    training can begin another chapter; an ordinary month or completed cycle can
    remain inside one. The programme that governed a session stays identifiable
    after the next revision.
  </p>
</Post>
